-- title:
--   Stabilising the exceptional valuation iff fixing the supersingular point (q=3)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ denotes `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`; for the intermediate field $k_0$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ introduced below, $A_0 :=$ `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))` is the valuation subring $A\cap k_0$ of $k_0$; and $B :=$ `chartAlgFin A₀ Kℓ jℓ` is the subalgebra of $K_\ell$ consisting of the elements integral over `Algebra.adjoin A₀ {jℓ}`, with distinguished element `jChartFin A₀ Kℓ jℓ` $= j_\ell$.
--
--   *Arithmetic data.* A prime $q$ with $q=3$; a nonzero $M'$ with $q\nmid M'$; a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g\equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g\mid M'$.
--
--   *Constants and reduction of constants.* A valuation subring $A$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ is a nonunit of $A$. A finite set $W$ of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` over the residue field of $A$ whose members are, by `hW`, exactly the elements of `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`: those places $w$ that are rational (the structure map into the residue field of $w$ is surjective), satisfy the predicate `IsAffineGeomPlace`, and whose value $w.\mathrm{evalAt}$ at the geometric $j$-generator lies in `ssJSet q`, the set of $j$ such that every elliptic curve with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$. The hypothesis `hle` requires `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` $\le$ `fieldBar q M'`, i.e. the base change to $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ of the full modular function field of level $M'$ lies in the base-changed function field of level `levelH q M'` inside $q^2M'$-Laurent series. A datum $R_0$ of type `ConstantReduction A (modularFunctionFieldBar M') (modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M')`: a valuation subring `R₀.integers` of the base-changed field, a surjective residue homomorphism `R₀.residue` onto `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` with kernel the maximal ideal, a degree-preserving map `R₀.placeMap` on places, the condition that a constant lies in `R₀.integers` exactly when it lies in $A$, compatibility of `R₀.residue` with the residue map of $A$ on constants, the scaling property `exists_smul_mem`, and the divisor compatibility `mapDomain_placeMap`. The hypothesis `hR₀` requires that for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\bar{\mathbb Q}$-Laurent series lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that element lies in `R₀.integers` and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over `ResidueField A`, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Finally $s$ is a chosen member of $W$, that is a supersingular place.
--
--   *The tame constant ring.* An intermediate field $k_0$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ and $\pi_0\in k_0$ whose image lies in $A$, subject to: `hdvr`, $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring; `hunif`, its maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$; `hhens`, $A_0$ is a henselian local ring; `hres`, the residue field of $A_0$ is algebraically closed; and `hκ`, every $a\in A$ is congruent to (the image of) some element of $k_0\cap A$ modulo the maximal ideal of $A$.
--
--   *Auxiliary prime, roots of unity, uniformisers.* A prime $\ell$ with $3\le \ell$, $\ell\neq q$, $\ell\nmid M'$; an element $\zeta_0\in k_0$ that is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity in $\bar{\mathbb Q}$; an element $\varpi_t\in k_0$ lying in $A$ with $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$ (`hϖt`). An intermediate field $K_b$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}/k_0$ with $K_b=\bot$, a valuation subring $A_b$ of $K_b$ whose elements are exactly those lying in $A$, and a nonzero generator $\varpi_b$ of the maximal ideal of $A_b$. The tameness hypothesis `htame`: $\ell_g-1$ is a unit of $A_0$. An element $\xi\in k_0$ that is a primitive $q$-th root of unity.
--
--   *Level structure and function field.* $H_1$ is the subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ equal to `levelH q M'` (the kernel of the unit reduction `ZMod.unitsMap (dvd_sq_mul q M')`) intersected with the kernel of the unit reduction to $(\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$. $K_\ell$ is the intermediate field of `LaurentSeries ↥k₀` over $k_0$ equal to `laurentBaseChange ↥k₀ (xHFunctionField (q^2*M') H₁)`, the $k_0$-compositum of the function field of level $H_1$ inside $q^2M'$; $A_0$ acts on $K_\ell$ compatibly with $k_0$. The element $j_\ell\in K_\ell$ has Laurent expansion `coeffEmb ↥k₀ jq`, the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion of $j$, and is nonzero. The element $\varpi_t'\in A_0$ has image $\varpi_t$ in $k_0$.
--
--   *The supersingular chart point.* $y$ is a maximal ideal of $B$ (`hy`) containing the image of $\pi_0$ (`hϖy`), and `hss` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi:B\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j_\ell)$ lies in `ssJSet q Ω`. The hypothesis `hover` states that $y$ lies over the place $s$: for every $g$ in the full modular function field of level $M'$ whose image lies in `R₀.integers`, which is regular wherever $j$ is (for every place $P$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, $\mathrm{ord}_P(j)\ge 0$ implies $\mathrm{ord}_P(g)\ge 0$) and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every witness that `coeffEmb ↥k₀ g` lies in $K_\ell$ and in $B$, and every $c\in k_0$ lying in $A$ whose residue in `ResidueField A` equals the value of $s$ at the $R_0$-residue of $g$, the element $g-c$ of $B$ lies in $y$. The hypothesis `hOrbit` states that every maximal ideal $y'$ of $B$ that contains the image of $\pi_0$ and satisfies this same lying-over condition is a translate of $y$: there are $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and a $k_0$-automorphism $\tau$ of $K_\ell$ with `IsLevelAutAt ↥k₀ q ξ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ Kℓ τ` such that $\tau$ maps $B$ into $B$ and, for $f\in B$ with $\tau f\in B$, $f\in y$ iff $\tau f\in y'$. Here `IsLevelAutAt ↥k₀ q ξ q N₀ H₁ δ Kℓ τ` says that for every weight $k$, every pair of modular forms $f,g$ of weight $k$ for [`CohCarrier.GammaH N₀ H₁`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ and $p_g$ of nonzero associated series, every $x\in K_\ell$ whose Laurent expansion is the embedded quotient of these series, and every ring homomorphism $\iota:k_0\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi)=\exp(2\pi i/q)$, the $\iota$-image of the expansion of $\tau x$ times the $q$-expansion of $g\mid_k(\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta)$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f\mid_k(\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta)$.
--
--   *The rigid chart and the exceptional valuation.* $B_t$ is an $A_0$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$, $W_t$ a valuation subring of $K_\ell$, with $B_t\subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hS1` is a conjunction of fourteen clauses, summarised here: $B\le B_t$; $K_\ell$ is the fraction field of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A_0$; $B_t/(\pi_0)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; the constants of $k_0$ lying in $W_t$ are exactly those coming from $A_0$; the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is generated by the image of $\pi_0$; $W_t$ is a discrete valuation ring; $y$ is the contraction to $B$ of the maximal ideal of $W_t$; $W_t$ is the localisation of $B_t$ at that maximal ideal; a Drinfeld coordinate-ring clause, requiring for every $\mathrm{GF}(q^2)$-algebra structure on the residue field $\kappa$ of $A_0$ a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho:B_t\to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q κ`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) whose zero set is the part of $B_t$ in the maximal ideal of $W_t$, which is compatible with the structure maps on $A_0$, which intertwines every level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ with [`DrinfeldCurve.hAction`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L325) at a pair $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma,c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276), with $c\neq 1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\neq\mathrm{id}$, and for which the same unit $c$ is realised by a further level automorphism attached to some $\gamma'\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ at the pair $(1,c)$; stability of $B_t$ under the level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma_0(M')$; the clause that every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ receives the centre of $W_t$ under some level automorphism attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$; and the clause that an element of $B_t$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W_t$ after every such level automorphism is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   *Order, crossing model and ends.* A natural number $n$ with $1\le n$ and $n\mid q+1$; an element $\gamma_0\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and a $k_0$-automorphism $\tau_0$ of $K_\ell$ with `IsLevelAutAt ↥k₀ q ξ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ₀⁻¹ Kℓ τ₀` which preserves $W_t$ (`hτ₀W`); a natural number $m$ with $1\le m$ and $\pi_0^m=\varpi_t'\,w$ for a unit $w$ of $A_0$; an element $\zeta_c$ of the adic completion of $A_0$ with $\zeta_c^n=1$ and $\zeta_c^k-1$ a unit for $0<k<n$; and a finite set $\mathrm{ends}$ of subrings of $K_\ell$. The hypothesis `hR7` is a conjunction of ten clauses, summarised here: $\tau_0^n$ fixes $B_t$ pointwise while $\tau_0^k$ does not for $0<k<n$; every level automorphism attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ that preserves $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with some $\tau_0^k$, $k<n$; $\mathrm{ends}$ has $q+1$ elements; the level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ permute $\mathrm{ends}$ and act transitively on it; $\tau_0$ preserves each member of $\mathrm{ends}$; distinct members $O\neq O'$ are separated by an element that is a nonunit of $O$ and a unit of $O'$; each $O\in\mathrm{ends}$ carries a local noetherian structure contained in $W_t$ and containing $B$, with constants exactly those from $A_0$ and residue field that of $A_0$, together with a crossing-model package — elements $c_x,c_y$ with $c_xc_y=\pi_0^m u$, an isomorphism $\iota$ of the adic completion of $O$ with `UVCrossingModel` of the adic completion of $A_0$ at $\pi_0^m$ (the quotient of $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}$ in two variables by $UV-\pi_0^m$) carrying constants to `UVCrossingModel.const`, $c_x$ and $c_y$ to unit multiples of $U$ and $V$, with $c_y$ in and $c_x$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, conjugating $\tau_0$ into an automorphism $\theta_0$ which acts on $U$ and $V$ by $\zeta_c$ and by its inverse $\zeta_c'$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal, and with a second valuation subring $W_x$ (the other branch at the crossing, with prescribed constants, uniformiser $\pi_0$, a transcendence clause for a parameter $t$, $c_x$ in and $c_y$ outside its maximal ideal, and a comparison of its centre with $y$) and a finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_x$ of $B_t\cap O$, stable under the level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, regular wherever $j_\ell$ is, and localising to $O$; and, finally, the clause that a valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ with constants exactly those from $A_0$, containing $B$ and with $y$ inside its centre, either contains $B_t$ or dominates some $O\in\mathrm{ends}$ in the stated sense. The hypothesis `hR5d` requires that every level automorphism $\tau$ attached to some $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $y$ on the chart (for $b\in B$ with $\tau b\in B$: $b\in y$ iff $\tau b\in y$) preserves $W_t$.
--
--   *Conclusion.* For every $k_0$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K_\ell$ such that there exists $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and `IsLevelAutAt ↥k₀ q ξ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ Kℓ τ`, the following two conditions are equivalent: first, $\tau$ preserves the exceptional valuation subring, that is for all $f\in K_\ell$ one has $f\in W_t$ if and only if $\tau f\in W_t$; second, $\tau$ fixes the chart point, that is for every $b\in B$ and every witness that $\tau b$ lies in $B$, one has $b\in y$ if and only if the element $\tau b$ of $B$ lies in $y$.
--
--   This is the rigidity step for the supersingular crossing point in the regular model of the modular curve of level $H_1$ over the tame constant ring $A\cap k_0$, in the $q=3$, $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-guarded frame: an automorphism attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ stabilises the exceptional valuation of the crossing exactly when it fixes the supersingular point of the $j$-chart. It is used in the computation of the order of the decomposition group of that valuation in terms of the number of rational automorphisms at the moduli place.
